@meta
id: 05-08
title: B and E, the coarse pair
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: WORD-motions, word-b, word-e
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: two clicks in a long expression, carefully
open: invoice.provider.ts

@brief
`w` `b` `e` all have capitals, and the capital always means the same
thing: ignore punctuation, stop only at whitespace.

    w  W   start of the next one
    b  B   start of the previous one
    e  E   end of this one

Two edits on line three, one in each direction.

The outstanding figure should use the gross total, so `invoice.total`
becomes `invoice.totalGross`. And credits now come off before payments,
so `credit.applied - ` goes in front of `payment.amount`. The line ends
up as:

    const outstanding = invoice.totalGross - credit.applied - payment.amount;

@keys
| `E` | forward to the end of the current or next WORD |
| `B` | back to the start of the previous WORD |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `a` | append after the cursor |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
The first edit appends to the end of something, the second inserts in
front of something. You already know which motion each of those pairs
with — this time use the capitals.

--
Going forward, `E` stops at the end of `const`, `outstanding`, `=`, and
then `invoice.total`. Four. Going back from the end of the line, `B`
reaches the start of `payment.amount;` in one.
--
`2j` `4E` then `a`, type `Gross`, `Esc`. Then `$` `B` `i`, type
`credit.applied - `, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    2j  4E  aGross<Esc>  $  B  i credit.applied -  <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`4E` walks the *ends* forwards; `$B` walks the *starts* backwards. Between
them you can reach either edge of any chunk on a line in two or three
keys, whatever punctuation is in it.

Note the second edit. From the end of the line, one `B` covered
`payment.amount;` entirely. With `b` it would have been four presses to
cross the same ground, and three of those landings were on characters
nobody wants to stand on.

The habit to build: decide which end of the target you need *before* you
move. Front of it means `b`/`B`/`w`/`W`. Back of it means `e`/`E`. Getting
that right is the difference between two keys and two keys plus a nudge.

@start invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public async summary(invoice: Invoice) {
    const outstanding = invoice.total - payment.amount;

    return { outstanding, currency: invoice.currency };
  }
}

@expected invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public async summary(invoice: Invoice) {
    const outstanding = invoice.totalGross - credit.applied - payment.amount;

    return { outstanding, currency: invoice.currency };
  }
}
